{%- comment -%}
  PG Cart Mobile: cart drawer fixes.

  1. The "Your Cart (n items)" bar stops sticking, so it scrolls with the list
     instead of riding over what is beneath it.

  2. The strip of cart peeking out under the pane is covered.
     The drawer carries an inline `padding-bottom` written by the theme's own
     script, and the pane sticks at `bottom:-10px`, so it settles short of the
     bottom edge with the cart showing through the difference.

     Covered by a skirt hung off the pane's bottom edge. It is PAINTED (a
     box-shadow spread) rather than laid out: as a 400px-tall element it was
     extending the drawer's scrollable height by 360px, which is the slab of
     blank white below CHECKOUT. Both the earlier box-shadow attempt and this
     one are written up by the rule -- read that note before touching it.

  3. The pane gets shorter under 760px: smaller paddings and gaps, one step
     down in type on the secondary rows, and the shipping-protection subtitle
     hidden (label, price and toggle all remain). The total and the checkout
     button are deliberately left large: they are what the pane is for.

  3b/3c. NO BLANK SLAB UNDER CHECKOUT, AT ANY WIDTH. Two things left dead space
     at the bottom of the pinned pane, and both used to be fixed on phones only:

       - the drawer is a flex column, so a cart too short to fill the screen
         drops its leftover height below the last child -- and the skirt from
         (2) paints that white. A flex spacer before the pane takes the slack
         instead. It was gated on max-width:760px; it is unconditional now, and
         is zero pixels tall whenever there is nothing to absorb.
       - the pane's own trailing padding and the CHECKOUT form's bottom margin.
         The margin survived because pg-drawer moves the payment strip into the
         pane as its last child and (4) hides it, so a `:last-child` rule was
         landing on a display:none node. The trailing margins are named
         explicitly now, and the pane's bottom padding is trimmed at every
         width rather than under 600px only.

  4. The payment-icon row is dropped from the pane at every width.

  5. The upsell card moves down on mobile only, to just above the pane.

  6. The pane is seated on the bottom edge -- MEASURED, not derived from the
     drawer's padding. See the long note by the code before touching it.

  NOT DONE, deliberately: unpinning the pane entirely. It was tried and it
  turned the drawer blank on iOS Safari. Removing the sticky element drops a
  compositing layer from a position:fixed, internally-scrolling container,
  which iOS renders as unpainted white.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
/* 1. header bar scrolls away, all widths */
#cart .pg-cart-top{
  position:static !important;
  top:auto !important;
  z-index:auto !important
}

/* 4. the payment-icon row comes out of the pinned pane, all widths. It is a
   wrapped strip of card-brand logos permanently on screen because the pane is
   pinned, saying what the "SECURE CHECKOUT" line above it already says. The
   cards still show at checkout itself and in the site footer. Scoped to the
   pane so the footer's strip is untouched. */
#cart .sticky-in-panel .l4pm{display:none !important}

/* 2. paint over the strip of cart showing below the pane, all widths.
   A skirt hung off the pane's bottom edge: no layout effect, and the drawer's
   own overflow clips it. `top:100%` is what makes it independent of the pane's
   height, which changes with the cart. */
#cart .sticky-in-panel{position:sticky}
#cart .sticky-in-panel::after{
  content:'';
  position:absolute;
  left:0;
  right:0;
  top:100%;
  /* 2b. THE SKIRT IS PAINTED, NOT LAID OUT -- AND THIS IS THE BLANK WHITE SLAB.

     It used to be a 400px-tall box with a white background. An absolutely
     positioned descendant extends its scroll container's scrollable overflow,
     and this one's container is the drawer, so every cart -- one line item or
     twelve -- carried 360px of scrollable, pure-white nothing below CHECKOUT.
     Measured on a 1280x900 window with two orbs: the drawer's content is 900px
     and its scrollHeight was 1260px; with the skirt suppressed, 900px and not
     scrollable at all. That void is the "blank white space at the bottom of the
     pane": you reach CHECKOUT and the drawer keeps going, into white.

     A box-shadow paints outside the border box without ever contributing to
     scrollable overflow, so the cover survives and the void does not. The note
     that a shadow was tried and rejected stands, and this is not that shadow:
     that one was `0 400px 0 0`, an OFFSET, which slid the whole shadow 400px
     down and left the strip immediately under CHECKOUT -- the only part that
     needed covering -- bare. This is a 400px SPREAD from a zero-height box
     pinned at the pane's bottom edge, which is the same thing `top:100%` was
     chosen for: it cannot get the pane's height wrong.

     clip-path keeps the spread below the box, since a spread grows in every
     direction. Where clip-path is unsupported the upward half paints behind the
     pane's rows -- `.m6pn .sticky-in-panel > *` carries z-index:1 and this
     pseudo does not -- so the worst case is invisible, not broken.

     Re-measured with this rule: scrollHeight 900px against a 900px client, and
     the pixel 20px below the pane still samples rgb(255,255,255). */
  height:0;
  background:none;
  box-shadow:0 0 0 400px #fff;
  clip-path:inset(0 0 -400px 0);
  pointer-events:none;
}

/* 9. SMOOTH OPEN (the drawer "pop-in"), all widths.
   The drawer slides in over 0.4s while its contents are still being built:
   the item rows fill out, the upsell lands, and the totals pane (Shipping
   Protection, You saved, Total, CHECKOUT) does not appear until ~0.7s and then
   settles for another few tenths. So the open is followed by a visible reshuffle.

   When the drawer opens with its content not yet assembled, the rows below the
   header are held invisible and faded in together once the pane has landed --
   see the JS. This trades a brief settling beat for a clean single reveal
   instead of a scramble. The header (close button, item count) stays visible
   throughout, the hold is capped hard in JS so nothing can stay hidden, and a
   re-open of an already-built cart skips the hold and is instant. */
#cart .nc-drawer-top,#cart .l4ca,
#cart #pg-unlock-slot,#cart .pg-unlock,#cart .sticky-in-panel{
  transition:opacity .14s ease;
}
#cart.pg-settling .nc-drawer-top,#cart.pg-settling .l4ca,
#cart.pg-settling #pg-unlock-slot,#cart.pg-settling .pg-unlock,
#cart.pg-settling .sticky-in-panel{
  opacity:0 !important;
}

/* 3b. PIN THE PANE -- AND THE CHECKOUT BUTTON WITH IT -- TO THE BOTTOM OF THE
   DRAWER ON A SHORT CART, AT EVERY WIDTH. #cart is a flex column
   (`.m6pn#cart{display:flex;flex-direction:column}`) and every child is
   flex:0 1 auto, so when the contents do not fill the screen the leftover space
   falls BELOW the last child, leaving a tall gap under CHECKOUT -- and the
   pane's own ::after skirt below paints that gap white, which is why it reads
   as a slab of blank white rather than as drawer ground.

   This is done with a FLEX SPACER (pg-cart-spacer, inserted by the JS just
   before the pane), NOT with margin-top:auto on the pane. An auto top margin
   was tried and it broke the pane's position:sticky on iOS Safari -- auto
   margins and sticky do not compose there -- so the pane stopped sticking and
   scrolled away with the list. The spacer keeps the pane's margins clean: it
   grows to swallow the free space above the pane on a short cart (pushing the
   pane to the bottom), and collapses to zero the moment the cart overflows and
   scrolls, leaving sticky to do its job untouched.

   IT USED TO BE PHONES ONLY, and the gap it fixes is not. The rule and its JS
   were both inside max-width:760px, so on a desktop drawer the free space still
   landed under CHECKOUT; pg-cart-total's `#cart .l4ca{flex-grow:1}` took most
   of it but only what the item list could absorb. The spacer is unconditional
   now: it is a zero-basis flex item, so where there is no free space it is
   zero pixels tall and changes nothing. */
#cart .pg-cart-spacer{flex:1 1 0;min-height:0;align-self:stretch;pointer-events:none}

/* 3c. THE PANE'S OWN TRAILING DEAD SPACE, AT EVERY WIDTH.

   pg-drawer's pgPane() moves the payment-icon strip (.l4pm) INTO the pane as
   its last child, and rule 4 above hides it. It is still the last element
   child, so `.sticky-in-panel > *:last-child{margin-bottom:0}` -- which
   pg-cart-fast applies under 600px -- lands on a display:none node and leaves
   the real last visible row, the CHECKOUT form, with its margin intact.

   So the trailing margins are named rather than selected positionally, and the
   pane's bottom padding is trimmed at every width instead of only on the
   narrowest phones. safe-area-inset-bottom is kept: it is the home-indicator
   allowance and is 0px on anything that does not need it. */
#cart .sticky-in-panel{padding-bottom:calc(6px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom)) !important}
/* and the trailing margin of whatever the drawer's own last child turns out to
   be -- the trust row once pgPane() has lifted the payment strip out of it.
   That margin is bare drawer ground below everything on screen, which is the
   same slab of white by another name. */
#cart > *:last-child{margin-bottom:0 !important}
#cart .sticky-in-panel > form.f8vl,
#cart .sticky-in-panel > form.f8vl > .link-btn,
#cart .sticky-in-panel > form.f8vl > .link-btn > *:last-child,
#cart .sticky-in-panel > .link-btn:last-child,
#cart .sticky-in-panel > *:last-child{margin-bottom:0 !important}

@media (max-width:760px){
  /* 3. compact the pane */
  #cart .sticky-in-panel{padding-top:3px !important}

  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sp{margin:2px 0 5px !important;padding:6px 9px !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sp-ic{width:24px !important;height:24px !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sp-ic svg{width:14px !important;height:14px !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sp-t b{font-size:12.5px !important;line-height:1.25 !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sp-t small{display:none !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sp-price{font-size:12.5px !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sp-sw{transform:scale(.9);transform-origin:right center}

  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sav{margin:2px 0 5px !important;padding:6px 9px !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sav-r{font-size:12px !important;line-height:1.4 !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .pg-sav-t{margin-top:3px !important;padding-top:4px !important;font-size:12.5px !important}

  #cart .sticky-in-panel .l4tt{margin:1px 0 !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .l4tt li{font-size:12.5px !important;padding:0 !important;line-height:1.35 !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .l4tt li.pg-drow,#cart .sticky-in-panel .l4tt li.pg-drow *{font-size:12.5px !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .l4tt li.pg-tt,#cart .sticky-in-panel .l4tt li.pg-tt *{font-size:15px !important}

  #cart .sticky-in-panel .nc-dtrust{margin:2px 0 !important;padding:0 !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel .nc-dtrust span{font-size:10px !important}

  #cart .sticky-in-panel form.f8vl{margin:2px 0 0 !important}
  #cart .sticky-in-panel button[name="checkout"]{padding:12px 16px !important}
}
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  if (window.pgCartFlush) return; window.pgCartFlush = 1;

  /* 5. on phones, drop the upsell down to just above the pinned pane, so it
     sits directly above Shipping Protection instead of in front of the cart.

     MOVE THE SLOT, NOT A CARD. pg-unlock now renders its offer rows inside a
     single container, #pg-unlock-slot, whose child order is pinned in CSS.
     This function used to grab `querySelector('.pg-unlock')` -- the FIRST
     offer row -- and move that one alone. With two offers in the cart (an orb
     and the console) each 60ms MutationObserver tick moved whichever row was
     currently first, so the two rows took turns being relocated and visibly
     SWAPPED about ten times a second. That was the mobile "spazzing".

     Moving the slot relocates both rows together as one unit, so there is
     nothing to reorder. The position check makes this safe under the observer:
     once the slot is already the pane's previous sibling there is nothing to
     do, so the move cannot feed itself. Falls back to a lone .pg-unlock if the
     slot is ever absent. */
  function placeUnlock(){
    var drawer = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (!drawer) return;
    var card = drawer.querySelector('#pg-unlock-slot') || drawer.querySelector('.pg-unlock');
    if (!card) return;

    if (window.matchMedia('(max-width:760px)').matches){
      var pane = drawer.querySelector('.sticky-in-panel');
      if (!pane || pane.previousElementSibling === card) return;
      if (!card.pgHome) card.pgHome = {parent: card.parentNode, next: card.nextSibling};
      pane.parentNode.insertBefore(card, pane);
    } else if (card.pgHome && card.pgHome.parent && card.pgHome.parent.isConnected){
      var home = card.pgHome;
      if (card.parentNode !== home.parent || card.nextSibling !== home.next){
        home.parent.insertBefore(card, home.next && home.next.isConnected ? home.next : null);
      }
    }
  }

  /* 6. SEAT THE PANE ON THE BOTTOM EDGE.
     The drawer carries an inline padding-bottom from the theme's own script,
     and the pane sticks at bottom:-10px, so it settles short of the bottom of
     the screen. Closing that hands the space back to the cart above it.

     The shift is MEASURED, never derived from that padding. An earlier version
     pulled the pane down by the padding value and broke the Instagram in-app
     browser: its bottom toolbar makes that padding far larger, and the Total
     and CHECKOUT button went off the bottom of the screen with it. Here the
     shift is the gap actually visible below the pane, measured against the
     visual viewport -- which excludes the browser's own toolbars -- so where
     nothing is visibly showing there is no shift, and it is capped regardless.

     Then it checks its work: if the checkout button is not fully on screen
     afterwards the shift is dropped. Nothing here can hide checkout.

     (The cold-cart checkout repair that used to live here now has its own
     file, snippets/pg-cart-morph-fix.liquid.) */
  var MAX_SHIFT = 44;
  function seatPane(){
    var drawer = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (!drawer) return;
    var pane = drawer.querySelector('.sticky-in-panel');
    if (!pane) return;

    /* always re-measure from the theme's own resting position */
    if (pane.pgBase == null){
      var b = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(pane).bottom);
      pane.pgBase = isNaN(b) ? 0 : b;
    }
    pane.style.removeProperty('bottom');

    var vh = (window.visualViewport && window.visualViewport.height) || window.innerHeight;
    var gap = vh - pane.getBoundingClientRect().bottom;
    if (!(gap > 1)) return;                       /* nothing visible below it */
    if (gap > MAX_SHIFT) gap = MAX_SHIFT;

    pane.style.setProperty('bottom', (pane.pgBase - gap) + 'px', 'important');

    var btn = drawer.querySelector('button[name="checkout"]');
    if (btn){
      var r = btn.getBoundingClientRect();
      if (r.bottom > vh + 1 || r.top < 0) pane.style.removeProperty('bottom');
    }
  }

  /* 7. the chooser button noun. pg-unlock's buildChooser hard-codes " orb" in
     the confirm button ("Add 1 orb to cart"), which is right for the orb but
     read as "ADD 1 ORB TO CART" on the PocketBoy R36 console upsell -- a
     console, not an orb. The correct fix is one line in pg-unlock, but that
     file is 34KB and the running theme cannot practically be rewritten through
     the API right now, so the label is corrected here from the row's handle:
     the orb and the print keep their noun, the console (or anything else) just
     says "Add N ... to cart". Idempotent, so when pg-unlock is next redeployed
     clean this simply finds nothing to change. */
  function fixLabels(){
    var drawer = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (!drawer) return;
    drawer.querySelectorAll('.pg-unlock').forEach(function(row){
      /* 7b. the offer NAME. pg-unlock's LADDERS still calls the free orb
         "Crystal PokeOrb", so the row reads "Add your FREE 3rd Crystal PokeOrb".
         The product is the Crystal Legends Orb -- rebrand it here (same reason
         as the noun fix: pg-unlock is 34KB and impractical to redeploy live).
         Fixed on the title element and idempotent. When pg-unlock is next
         redeployed clean (its LADDERS name is already updated in source) this
         finds nothing to change. */
      var ttl = row.querySelector('.pg-unlock-t b') || row.querySelector('.pg-unlock-t');
      if (ttl && /PokeOrb/i.test(ttl.textContent)){
        ttl.textContent = ttl.textContent
          .replace(/Crystal\s+PokeOrb/gi, 'Crystal Legends Orb')
          .replace(/PokeOrb/gi, 'Legends Orb');
      }
      var go = row.querySelector('.pg-unlock-go');
      if (!go) return;
      var h = row.getAttribute('data-pg-unlock') || '';
      if (h.indexOf('pokemon-crystal') > -1 || h.indexOf('anime-pokemon') > -1) return;
      var t = go.textContent;
      var fixed = t.replace(/\s+orb\b/i, '').replace(/\s+print\b/i, '');
      if (fixed !== t) go.textContent = fixed;
    });
  }

  /* 8. the Shipping Protection switch getting stuck. pg-drawer's toggle sets
     data-busy on the .pg-sp box (which CSS dims and makes pointer-events:none)
     and only clears it on ERROR -- on success it relies on the whole drawer
     being rebuilt to throw the box away. pg-cart-smooth's morph now PRESERVES
     that box instead of rebuilding it, so data-busy is never removed and the
     switch stays dimmed and unclickable after the first on/off.

     Cleared here. apply() runs on the childList mutation the refresh produces,
     which is exactly when the operation has completed, so the switch frees the
     moment the add/remove lands. The box's checkbox already reflects the new
     cart state, so nothing else needs syncing. */
  function unstickSP(){
    var drawer = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (!drawer) return;
    drawer.querySelectorAll('.pg-sp[data-busy]').forEach(function(b){ b.removeAttribute('data-busy'); });
  }

  /* 3b (cont). Keep a flex spacer sitting directly before the pane so a short
     cart's free space collapses ABOVE the pane instead of leaving white under
     CHECKOUT. It is a plain element with no margins, so the pane's sticky
     positioning is left untouched (see the CSS note).

     EVERY WIDTH, not just phones. This was gated on max-width:760px and removed
     the spacer on desktop, which is where the blank slab under the pane was
     still showing: pg-cart-total's `#cart .l4ca{flex-grow:1}` hands the free
     space to the item list, but only as much as that list as a flex child of
     #cart can take, and anything left over lands under the pane with the
     ::after skirt painting it white. Ungated it costs nothing where there is no
     free space -- flex:1 1 0 resolves to zero height -- and it is removed only
     when the pane itself is gone. */
  function placeSpacer(){
    var drawer = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (!drawer) return;
    var pane = drawer.querySelector('.sticky-in-panel');
    var spacer = drawer.querySelector('.pg-cart-spacer');
    if (!pane){
      if (spacer && spacer.parentNode) spacer.parentNode.removeChild(spacer);
      return;
    }
    if (!spacer){
      spacer = document.createElement('div');
      spacer.className = 'pg-cart-spacer';
      spacer.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    }
    /* must be the pane's immediate previous sibling */
    if (pane.previousElementSibling !== spacer){
      pane.parentNode.insertBefore(spacer, pane);
    }
  }

  function apply(){ placeUnlock(); placeSpacer(); seatPane(); fixLabels(); unstickSP(); }

  /* 9. SMOOTH OPEN. The theme adds `m6pn-open` to <html> when the drawer opens.
     If the cart's totals pane is not rendered yet at that moment, the drawer is
     opening onto content that is still assembling, so hold the rows invisible
     (CSS class pg-settling) and clear it -- fading them in -- the instant the
     totals pane stops moving (its position matches across two 50ms polls). That
     is the earliest CLEAN moment: revealing before it settles trades the
     reshuffle for a smaller post-reveal nudge, which is still a jump, so the
     reveal is pinned to the settle instead. On a fast connection that is ~0.7s;
     the poll is quick so there is no slack beyond it. A hard cap and a
     close-resets guard mean the rows can never be stuck hidden, and a cart that
     is already built when it opens skips the hold entirely (instant re-open). */
  function smoothOpen(){
    var drawer = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (!drawer || drawer.classList.contains('pg-settling')) return;
    if (drawer.querySelector('.sticky-in-panel')) return;   /* already built -- instant */
    drawer.classList.add('pg-settling');
    var start = Date.now(), lastTop = null, stable = 0;
    (function wait(){
      var d = document.getElementById('cart');
      if (!d) return;
      if (!d.classList.contains('toggle')){ d.classList.remove('pg-settling'); return; }
      var pane = d.querySelector('.sticky-in-panel');
      var done = Date.now() - start > 1200;   /* safety only -- stability reveals first */
      if (pane){
        var top = Math.round(pane.getBoundingClientRect().top);
        if (top === lastTop) stable++; else { stable = 0; lastTop = top; }
        if (stable >= 1) done = true;
      }
      if (done){ d.classList.remove('pg-settling'); return; }
      setTimeout(wait, 50);
    })();
  }

  function boot(){
    apply();
    window.addEventListener('resize', apply);
    if (window.visualViewport) window.visualViewport.addEventListener('resize', apply);
    /* watch the drawer's own class: the theme adds `toggle` to #cart when it
       opens. Firing off that (rather than the <html> open class) is the reliable
       hook -- the reveal starts the instant the drawer opens, before the
       reshuffle can show. */
    var drawerEl = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (window.MutationObserver && drawerEl){
      new MutationObserver(function(){
        if (drawerEl.classList.contains('toggle')) smoothOpen();
        else drawerEl.classList.remove('pg-settling');
      }).observe(drawerEl, {attributes:true, attributeFilter:['class']});
      if (drawerEl.classList.contains('toggle')) smoothOpen();
    }
    /* the theme replaces the drawer contents wholesale on every add, remove and
       quantity change, and an add-to-cart round trip outruns fixed timers, so
       watch for the re-render and re-apply. childList only. */
    if (window.MutationObserver){
      var queued = false;
      new MutationObserver(function(){
        if (queued) return;
        queued = true;
        setTimeout(function(){ queued = false; apply(); }, 60);
      }).observe(document.body, {childList:true, subtree:true});
    }
    [400, 1000, 2200, 4500].forEach(function(t){ setTimeout(apply, t); });
  }

  if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', boot);
  else boot();
})();
</script>
{% schema %}
{"name": "PG Cart Mobile", "settings": []}
{% endschema %}
